1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:29,980
God bless you.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:58,000
I hope you all are doing well. We have a stage prop that we are preparing, which is, you know, always an exciting time, and it takes longer to do than we thought it was going to take, so that's what's happening right now. But I'm going to trust the process, put the laptop down for a second, and let you know that we are starting a brand new sermon series. How many of you enjoyed the Revelation series?

3
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Awesome. That is one. I thanked my wife last week because if it was not for her, I don't know that that series ever would have happened. I've been avoiding the book of Revelation for a very long time, based on my own baggage and misunderstandings. My wife encouraged me to read it with her, and out of that came a really just a fresh perspective and an excitement about the truths that we talked about in the book of Revelation.

4
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:47,000
So last week, Kent kind of wrapped up the series, put a bow on it, reminded us of the prevailing message of the book, that the lamb who looks like he's slain, but sounds like a lion has conquered. The dragon is flailing. In this world, we will have trouble, but take heart for Christ has overcome the world.

5
00:01:47,000 --> 00:02:11,000
And this fall, we're going to be wrapping up a series that we've been doing for about three years now. If you've been around Canvas for that long, thank you. God bless you. And you will know that we've been in a series through nine practices through the life and teachings of Jesus. So far, we've covered prayer, fasting, Sabbath, solitude, generosity, and community.

6
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Would you do me a favor? And if you have any lasting practical applications from any of those practices, if you're still doing something that you started because of one of those series, would you raise your hand for me? Awesome. All right.

7
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:37,000
So we've got some practical applications, some continuing application. And really, that is the goal of these practices, because following Jesus is not about learning more information.

8
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:44,000
It's about obeying Jesus with the information that we have. Yes, we should learn, but if we're not obeying, then we're missing the point.

9
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:51,000
The whole series is wrapped around this statement that we are called to be with, become like, and do what Jesus did.

10
00:02:51,000 --> 00:03:00,000
And we're called to do that in the context of community, that obedience to Jesus in isolation is also missing the mark for what Jesus has for us.

11
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:09,000
The final two practices that we have left are witness and Scripture. We decided to do witness first for two reasons.

12
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:14,000
One, I think, is a very natural application from the message of the Revelation series.

13
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:32,000
If we have been convinced that Jesus has been established, that the dragon is counterfeiting his neighbors and coworkers into believing a message that is less than the gospel of Jesus,

14
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:36,000
then a natural outpouring of that understanding would be that we would want to testify.

15
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,000
We would want to witness to the truth that Jesus has overcome.

16
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:47,000
We want to kind of come out of the Revelation series encouraging us to think about how we can creatively, intentionally talk to our friends about the kingdom of God.

17
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:58,000
And secondly, we are going to be having a training at the end of this series in October that we would love for you to think about participating in.

18
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:07,000
This series is a practical training on how we can live missionally, how we can engage with our friends and our community with the gospel of Jesus in a way that isn't weird.

19
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:12,000
So that's kind of the elevator pitch. Check out the website. It's got more information.

20
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:17,000
We've got dinner and childcare provided. Pastor Sam from Hope Valley is going to be helping us.

21
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:23,000
The Hope Valley Church is going to be joining us. So it's going to be a two church congregation training.

22
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:32,000
And so we would love for you to consider, pray about joining us for that. Even if you can't do all six weeks, if your schedule lets you do four, whatever, like come hang out with us.

23
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:36,000
It'll be great. Sound good? That's where we're headed this fall. Let's pray and we'll hop in.

24
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:47,000
Father, we love you. And we thank you that you love us and that you love us so much that we can multitask while we're praying on stage to set up our laptop.

25
00:04:47,000 --> 00:05:05,000
That's so kind of you. You're so good. We thank you for this opportunity to open up your word, to think about how we can creatively engage people who are outside,

26
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:14,000
who have not yet tasted and seen that you are good and that your mercy endures forever, that we can think about how we can be creative witnesses,

27
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:23,000
citizens of your kingdom until you return. Give us clarity and wisdom. Holy Spirit, just open up our eyes to see where you might be using us

28
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:31,000
or wanting to use us in these ways. For your glory, our good, and the good of our neighbor, it's in Jesus' name I pray. Amen.

29
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:40,000
All right, I do not have a ton of vivid memories from my childhood, but there is one in particular as I was preparing for this message that I could not forget.

30
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:45,000
Hopefully, this doesn't trigger warning, Mom. This is going to bring some things back for me and you.

31
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:50,000
I remember a time in which my mother and I got food poisoning from. Mom?

32
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:58,000
No, you're wrong. Kentucky Fried Chicken. She's wrong. I'm right. Don't worry about it. Trust it.

33
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:07,000
She says, "McDonald's. I say Kentucky Fried Chicken." Either way, it happened, and we were double-dipped. We have one bathroom in our house.

34
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:14,000
So she was puking in the tub. I was puking in the toilet. We shared some...we bond it.

35
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:26,000
And for literally about 15 years after that, I...and this is why I know that I'm right, because I would not touch Kentucky Fried Chicken for most of my life after this experience.

36
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:32,000
She's over here convincing her row that I'm wrong, and she's right. It's fine. It's fine.

37
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:39,000
But as I share this, you probably have one of your own stories, something similar to this, whether it's Kentucky Fried Chicken,

38
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:49,000
McDonald's, Taco Bell, coleslaw, oysters, tequila. I don't know what it is for you. Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa.

39
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:54,000
Real people realize. I didn't say it was a good thing. I just said it happens.

40
00:06:54,000 --> 00:07:01,000
These experiences often leave us convinced that something that is either good or neutral is bad, right?

41
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:10,000
We have this negative experience with something that's either positive or neutral, and it convinces us for however long that that thing is not a good thing.

42
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:17,000
And I think this is exactly what has happened for many of us, whether we've grown up in the church or experienced the church from the outside.

43
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:25,000
When it comes to this idea of Christian witness or the church word that we use is evangelism, when I say that word, do any of you cringe?

44
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:33,000
Okay, some of us have this baggage where we've received something that is good in a bad way. We've seen it done poorly.

45
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:38,000
We've been pressured into doing something that maybe felt uncomfortable or unnatural to us.

46
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:44,000
And this is my experience with evangelism. I remember as a kid, like being in a church van, going door to door,

47
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:52,000
just in a way that I was not comfortable with, to people that I've never met, getting like as an 11 or 12 year old,

48
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:57,000
getting the door slammed in your face by like a big burly dude, it's not a good thing for me, right?

49
00:07:57,000 --> 00:08:02,000
And so like I carried some of this baggage with me into Bible college, so much so that I thought I was a bad Christian,

50
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:06,000
because the idea of evangelism did not excite me at all. Like I didn't want to do it, and I was like,

51
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:12,000
maybe I'm not a Christian, because Christians should want to tell people about Jesus, and I do not, right?

52
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:19,000
I had this experience with this good thing in a bad way, and it shaped the way that I handled it.

53
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:22,000
Or maybe you've been on the outside. You've been on the receiving end of this.

54
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:30,000
You got one of those $100 bill tracks instead of a tip. You got yelled at by an angry street preacher.

55
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:39,000
You've been treated like a project or a lead for someone's spiritual sales quota, rather than a person to be loved and listened to.

56
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:54,000
So some of us carry some baggage, whether inside or outside the church. Not only that, but now we live in a postmodern, pluralistic society that has told us that sharing any ultimate truth claim can be offensive to somebody that you're talking to.

57
00:08:54,000 --> 00:09:01,000
In a recent poll by the Barner Group, they studied millennial Christians.

58
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:06,000
96% of millennial Christians say this, "Part of my faith means being a witness about Jesus."

59
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:12,000
So almost 100% of Christians say, millennial Christians say, "I need to be telling others about Jesus."

60
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:17,000
94% say the best thing that could ever happen to someone is to know Jesus.

61
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:26,000
So 96% convinced that we're called to tell others. 94% convinced that if they do trust Jesus, it'll be the best thing that ever happens to them.

62
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:35,000
But 47% of those same people also say it is wrong to share one's personal beliefs with someone of a different faith

63
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:42,000
and hopes that they will one day share the same faith. So we've got baggage from inside the church, baggage from outside the church,

64
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:48,000
and a culture that is telling us that it is offensive to tell somebody about Jesus.

65
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:54,000
So all of that to say, if you're feeling uncomfortable about this practice, about witnessing, testifying,

66
00:09:54,000 --> 00:10:02,000
sharing the message of Jesus with people all around you, I just want you to take a deep breath and realize that you're not alone.

67
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:08,000
But what if the solution to bad evangelism isn't no evangelism?

68
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:17,000
What if the call for us is actually to reclaim a better motive, a clearer message, and a better manner in which we go about evangelizing,

69
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:25,000
or telling people, or witnessing to the kingdom of heaven until Jesus returns?

70
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:29,000
So this is what we want to do for the next four weeks.

71
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:34,000
We want to reclaim this idea of what good, beautiful, joyful evangelism looks like.

72
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:39,000
And the definition that we're working with when it comes to evangelism or witness is we're going to define it this way,

73
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:46,000
that we are called to demonstrate and declare the reign of King Jesus wherever we live, work, and play.

74
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:53,000
We want to demonstrate and declare. So we want to talk about the kingdom of God, and we want to embody the kingdom of God.

75
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:57,000
Two people that are in our circles of influence where we live, work, and play.

76
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:01,000
And there's other ways in which we can witness to the gospel of Jesus.

77
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:04,000
Some of you might be called to become a missionary.

78
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:09,000
That might mean you actually get up and move and go to a people group that you don't know.

79
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:12,000
That's a beautiful and good thing, but we can't cover all of that in one series.

80
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:16,000
So with this series, we're talking about where you are right now, where you live, work, and play.

81
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:20,000
How do you demonstrate and declare the kingdom of God?

82
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:32,000
So let's try and leave our baggage at the door and reclaim the joy of sharing the best news that we've ever received.

83
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:37,000
I do want to also mention that in this series, another hopefully deep breath moment for you,

84
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:41,000
is that we're not asking you to add a bunch of extra things to your schedule.

85
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
We're not asking you to add a bunch of things to your list.

86
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:46,000
We're asking you to be intentional with the things you're already doing.

87
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:50,000
It's the emphasis on live, work, and play. All of you live, work, and play somewhere.

88
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:57,000
There are likely people in your sphere of influence, where you live, where you work, where you play, that do not know Jesus.

89
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:05,000
So what are some intentional ways that you can reclaim those spaces in order to demonstrate and declare the kingdom of God?

90
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:09,000
Sound good? All right. So for the rest of our time, we're going to open our Bibles.

91
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:15,000
We're eventually going to land in Colossians chapter 2. I'm going to reference some other passages of Scripture along the way.

92
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:24,000
We're going to talk about motive, message, and manner to demonstrating and declaring the rule and reign of Jesus where we live, work, and play.

93
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:30,000
Did everybody track and sound like a good plan? All right. Perfect. I didn't lose you with the vomit story.

94
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:40,000
I just had to bring it back up for those of you that are just getting over it. Oh, man. We brought it back up. All right.

95
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:50,000
First, motive. Our motivation, the reason why we go about demonstrating and declaring the kingdom of God is where we have to start.

96
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:58,000
And it's oftentimes where people totally get off the rails. It's where people miss the opportunity to do the thing joyfully that God has invited them to do.

97
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:08,000
Because I will say this, I will say this way, why we do what we do drastically impacts how we do it. Why we do what we do drastically impacts how we do it.

98
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:17,000
And if we come to this idea of witness with a bad motivation, or we've been trained by a bad motivation, it will impact the way in which we go about doing this.

99
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:25,000
And there's a number of bad motivations that I've experienced, maybe some of you have experienced, with the idea of Christian witness.

100
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:31,000
The first is treating people like a sales lead for your spiritual quota.

101
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:45,000
I will never forget in college, I went to this, I won't name the ministry that does it, but it's like this overnight thing where you bring a bunch of youth kids and you play and you get them like really sleep deprived.

102
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:51,000
And then you tell them that they're going to hell if they don't like recite this chant after you.

103
00:13:51,000 --> 00:14:01,000
Some of you've experienced that before. And I'll never forget though, we were, it was like midnight on the, the dude, the preacher was on the ice at a hockey rink.

104
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:11,000
And he was like, last year, this time we preached the gospel and one of these kids wanted to get saved and they came down here and they slipped on the ice and cracked their head open and they died that night.

105
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:20,000
What if that happens to you? Do you want to go to hell or do you want to go to heaven? And I was just like, we're doing it. We're doing the thing, right?

106
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:27,000
And again, like these are brothers and sisters in Christ who love the Lord, but the motivation is wrong, right? They're treating people like a sales quota.

107
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:41,000
Whatever motivation I can do, whatever way I can weasel myself in, I can come up with a presentation to scare you or to manipulate you or to whatever, to get you to check a box so that I can go home to my church and say, "Guess what? We had 500 kids get saved at this thing."

108
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:51,000
Did we have 500 kids get saved or were 500 kids scared to death, right? And can the Lord work through that? And do I know people who are pastors because they got saved in those moments? Sure.

109
00:14:51,000 --> 00:15:03,000
But bad motives lead to bad witness, and it carries with us baggage. And the answer to bad evangelism is not no evangelism, right?

110
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:15,000
So there's sales leads. There's trying to prove a stranger wrong. Sometimes that's out of insecurity. Like, I want to convince myself that what I believe is right, and so I'm going to get in a dialogical debate with a stranger,

111
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:24,000
and I'm going to be angry and irritable about it because I want to prove to myself that I'm right as much as I want to prove to that other person that they're right. Guilt for not doing it.

112
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:32,000
If you grew up in the church, I had this. Like, sometimes I would literally, like, force myself to have a conversation with somebody about Jesus because I felt guilty if I didn't.

113
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:44,000
And that motivation is going to drastically impact how I go about doing that. That person in front of me is going to see right through the fact that I'm not talking to them about a kingdom of heaven

114
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:51,000
because I love them. I want them to experience the joy of who Jesus is. I'm talking to them because I feel guilty about not talking to them.

115
00:15:51,000 --> 00:16:01,000
So why we do what we do drastically impacts how we do it. And if this series, if this whole series is about being with, becoming like, and doing what Jesus did,

116
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:08,000
then the question becomes, what was Jesus's motivation for announcing, for demonstrating and declaring the kingdom of God?

117
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:26,000
I think a survey of the Gospels, the Gospels being the culmination of the entire biblical story, would seem to indicate that love and compassion is the primary reason Jesus came to demonstrate and declare the good news of his kingdom to the lost, hurting, and broken.

118
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:36,000
We know the verses, "For God so loved the world that he gave his one and only Son, who came not to condemn the world, but to save the world through him.

119
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:53,000
The Son of Man came seeking and saving the lost." The motivation at which Jesus came, the motivation in which God moved towards us even in creation before the fall, God moving towards humanity, breathing the breath of God into the lungs of Adam and Eve, that is love, God's love moving towards us in relationship.

120
00:16:53,000 --> 00:17:04,000
This is the motivation of God that we see ultimately demonstrated through the incarnation of Jesus. And there's one Greek word that comes up over and over again in the Gospels,

121
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:14,000
and it's only used as a motivation and emotion that Jesus feels towards people. It's not ever used of other people feeling towards other people.

122
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:22,000
You guys want to learn some Greek? Apparently, this is when you get in real weird Bible nerd circles.

123
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:32,000
Apparently, there's been some recent research done on how Koine Greek is a dead language. So we're reconstructing how to pronounce it.

124
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:39,000
And there's a way in which I was taught to pronounce it in seminary. And now, apparently, there's a debate about whether or not that's pronounced right or not.

125
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:45,000
Guess what? Doesn't matter. So we're going to pronounce it the way I know how to pronounce it. And if it's wrong, it's wrong.

126
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:53,000
Can you look at your neighbor and say, "Splakizomai." "Splakizomai." "Splakizomai." There's a little bit of, you know, in the throat there.

127
00:17:53,000 --> 00:18:00,000
This is a word, again, only ever used of Jesus towards people. It's translated in most of your translations as compassion.

128
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:09,000
And literally, it is an emotion that Jesus feels towards people. It's a gut-level emotion that changes your behavior.

129
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:18,000
A gut-level emotion that actually changes the direction of your behavior. The clearest way I can illustrate this is in Mark.

130
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:25,000
When Jesus had just had a really busy ministry season with him as his disciples, they were very exhausted.

131
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:30,000
And he said, "Come away with me. For a little while, we're going to rest." Okay?

132
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:34,000
So Jesus is like, "We're exhausted. We need to rest. We're going away from the crowds."

133
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:37,000
They get in the boat. They go away from the crowds. They get off the boat. And guess what?

134
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:45,000
The crowds beat them to the place that they were going to rest. And it says in the text that Jesus had "Splachizomai."

135
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:52,000
He had compassion on them. And his compassion changed his behavior. He had planned to go rest, and now he is going to perform

136
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:59,000
one of the most probably draining miracles of all time, turning a couple of loaves and a couple of fish

137
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:07,000
and enough food to feed 20,000 people. This is the compassion that Jesus has on people who are lost

138
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:12,000
and who are like sheep without a shepherd. And this is the motivation that I'm going to argue that we need to cultivate

139
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:18,000
within our own hearts if we're going to ever be able to testify, to demonstrate, and to declare the kingdom of God

140
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:24,000
to people where we live, work, and play. We have to start with a gut-level compassion for people who are

141
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:32,000
being, again, back to Revelation, deceived by the dragon to believe a lie that is leading them to anxiety, depression,

142
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:39,000
fear, sadness, sorrow, whatever experience they might be having. We have to start with compassion for them

143
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:44,000
and not compassion for them like, "I'm better than you, and if you just knew what I knew, then you wouldn't experience

144
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:50,000
the things you're experiencing." No genuine, like, heartfelt compassion for people who don't yet know,

145
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:56,000
who have not yet tasted and seen that the Lord is good. So this is the motivation that Jesus has for us,

146
00:19:56,000 --> 00:20:02,000
and it translates to his early missionaries. In 2 Corinthians 5, we see the Apostle Paul,

147
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:08,000
one of the early church's first official missionaries, being motivated by the same motivation.

148
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:19,000
In 2 Corinthians 5, it says, "Either way, Christ's love controls us." This is Paul's motivation in his missionary journey.

149
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:28,000
It's Paul's motivation in his ministry to people. His love, the love of Christ for him and for the people he's ministering to controls him.

150
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:36,000
Paul had become convinced of God's love for him, first and foremost. He says, "I'm the chief among sinners,

151
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:43,000
and yet God loved me, saved me, redeemed me, has given me a fresh start." So first, if we're going to have compassion for others,

152
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:48,000
we have to realize how much God loves us. Charlie talked about this in his testimony last week.

153
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:53,000
We're supposed to love the Lord your God with all your heart, soul, mind, and strength and love your neighbor as yourself.

154
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:58,000
If you don't love yourself, you can't love your neighbor in the way that God intended you to love your neighbor.

155
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:04,000
We've got to realize how much God has loved us. We have to allow the Father to parent us and to love us

156
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:09,000
unconditionally, and then out of the overflow of that love, receiving that love,

157
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:20,000
we move towards people who have yet to taste and see the Lord is good, and we do it with compassion.

158
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:31,000
This is a model for how we can begin to, like Jesus and Paul, move with compassion towards people in the circles of influence,

159
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:35,000
where they haven't yet understood or responded to Christ's love for them.

160
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:39,000
We used to have this saying at my old church in Pennsylvania. I love my old church in Pennsylvania.

161
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:44,000
Still chat with them on a regular basis. We just talk about people who are far from God.

162
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:48,000
I don't like that language anymore. I understand what it's trying to communicate,

163
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:54,000
but the people that don't yet know Jesus, where you live, work, and play, they're not far from God.

164
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:59,000
God is in their midst. He is at work. He is in the breath that they are consuming in their lungs.

165
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:02,000
They just haven't yet tasted and seen that he's good, but he is there.

166
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:11,000
He is waiting for you to demonstrate and declare his rule and reign to each one of those people because God so loved the world.

167
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:19,000
So we meditate on God's love, and we pray for God to give us genuine compassion for those in our lives.

168
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:26,000
If we want to be a joyful witness, if we want to do this not out of guilt or shame or meeting a sales quota

169
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:33,000
or whatever motivation it might be, if we want to do this well, it has to start with compassion for people.

170
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:39,000
But we also have to believe the message that we are called to share is actually good news.

171
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:46,000
Which leads me to my second point. The motive is compassion, and we have to get clear about what the message really is.

172
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:53,000
I think another reason why some of us might have negative reactions around the idea of evangelism, witness, sharing the gospel,

173
00:22:53,000 --> 00:23:01,000
is we've been given a watered-down and oversimplified version of the gospel that often sounds more like bad or scary news than good news.

174
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:08,000
It's this, "You might die tomorrow. Do you want to be in hell, or do you want to be in heaven?"

175
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:17,000
Is that an implication of the gospel? Yes, absolutely. I'm not saying we alter the message of Jesus, but we've been given this message.

176
00:23:17,000 --> 00:23:22,000
We've called it the gospel, and it's a very small sliver of the pie of what Jesus did when he announced,

177
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:31,000
and when he demonstrated, and when he declared the kingdom of God. I preached on this in October of 2024.

178
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:36,000
I preached the message called, "What is the gospel?" I would love for you to go back and check that out,

179
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:44,000
because I don't have time to do all of it this morning. But what I would say is the message of the gospel

180
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:50,000
that we've been called to demonstrate and declare is so much more than about where you go when you die.

181
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:58,000
It's about Jesus overcoming death and the grave, and establishing his kingdom on earth until he returns,

182
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:05,000
and we are citizens of that kingdom that get to participate in the life-giving, abundant life

183
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:13,000
that comes with knowing that death is not the end, and that sin and grave will be conquered once and for all.

184
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:18,000
The message of Jesus is so much better than about where you go when you die.

185
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:27,000
And we see this in Luke's account of the story of Jesus. There's this progression in Luke 8, Luke 9, Luke 10, and all the way down to Acts.

186
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:34,000
So just let me read it down, follow along with me. In Luke's account, in the story of Jesus, in Luke 8:1,

187
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:40,000
it says that Jesus went around proclaiming and preaching the kingdom of God. This is the gospel.

188
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:46,000
This is the message that Jesus went around proclaiming, the message of the kingdom of God. Not the message of where we go

189
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:52,000
when we die, not the message of condemnation for sin, the message of the kingdom of God, that Jesus has

190
00:24:52,000 --> 00:25:00,000
conquered sin and death, and that he is risen and reigning and ruling until he returns. So in Luke 8, Jesus

191
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:07,000
proclaims and preaches the kingdom. It also says in that text that many of his earliest followers were

192
00:25:07,000 --> 00:25:14,000
following him because he met their practical needs. Read this with me. Also, some women who had been cured

193
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:20,000
of evil spirits and diseases. So like not only is he declaring this message that he has conquered

194
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:27,000
sin, death, and the grave, that he is overcoming the travesty of the brokenness of this world, but he's

195
00:25:27,000 --> 00:25:32,000
demonstrating it through miraculous signs and wonders. He's showing up, and he's showing people

196
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:41,000
what the kingdom of God is like. In chapter 9, he gives his apostles, his disciples, power and authority

197
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:46,000
over demons and to heal the sick, and tells them to go and proclaim the kingdom of God

198
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:51,000
and to perform healing. So now he's, first he's doing it, then he's sending out 12 of his

199
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:56,000
disciples to do the same thing, both demonstrate and declare the rule of King Jesus

200
00:25:56,000 --> 00:26:03,000
on earth until he returns. And then in Luke 10, he does it again with 72 of his followers.

201
00:26:03,000 --> 00:26:08,000
And finally, we get to Acts chapter 1, his last words before ascending into heaven. He

202
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:13,000
commissions his followers who are there in the room, and by extension, all of us who

203
00:26:13,000 --> 00:26:22,000
have bowed the knee to King Jesus, and he commissions us to be his witnesses. He gives us power by the

204
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:30,000
Holy Spirit to both demonstrate and declare God's kingdom to the world around us, to be a witness to

205
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:37,000
the kingdom of God in both demonstration and declaration, wherever we live, work, and play.

206
00:26:37,000 --> 00:26:44,000
And this commission that he gave to his early followers, it wasn't a foreign concept to

207
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:52,000
them. This was a familiar concept that they understood, the idea of being witness. I'm

208
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:56,000
forgetting the Greek word now. It sounds like martyr, because that's where we get the word

209
00:26:56,000 --> 00:27:00,000
martyr, because the earliest witnesses were killed for testifying to the kingdom of Jesus.

210
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:05,000
I think it's Martis or something like that. This idea of being a witness was something that

211
00:27:05,000 --> 00:27:10,000
regularly happened in the Roman Empire. The Roman Empire had witnesses who would go

212
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:15,000
around from town to town, and they would declare, they would announce the good news

213
00:27:15,000 --> 00:27:20,000
of a Roman military victory, of some kind of conquest, of some kind of peace being restored

214
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:25,000
in the empire. So they had these witnesses that would go about, and literally the same

215
00:27:25,000 --> 00:27:32,000
Greek word for gospel is the word used by Josephus to explain what these Roman

216
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:37,000
witnesses would do. They would go, and they would declare a euangelion, a gospel, a

217
00:27:37,000 --> 00:27:44,000
good news. And they would say, "Here is the good news of the Roman Empire. We need you

218
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:50,000
to metanoia, repent, and pistis believe." Literally the same gospel structure, the way

219
00:27:50,000 --> 00:27:55,000
that Jesus proclaimed the gospel, the way that he invited us to proclaim the gospel. Because

220
00:27:55,000 --> 00:28:01,000
they were proclaiming temporary good news and inviting their citizens to be good

221
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:07,000
citizens of the Roman Empire. And this is exactly what the early Christian movement was

222
00:28:07,000 --> 00:28:11,000
doing. They were saying, "Listen, the Roman Empire is fine, but it's temporary. It's going to

223
00:28:11,000 --> 00:28:16,000
fall apart. There's a kingdom. There's a new king who sits on his throne. You say Caesar

224
00:28:16,000 --> 00:28:22,000
is Lord. We say Jesus is Lord, and his lordship is eternal. And we are here to testify to the euangelion

225
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:28,000
that not only is there temporary peace, but there is eternal peace because Jesus has conquered sin,

226
00:28:28,000 --> 00:28:33,000
death, and the grave at his resurrection. And because of that, if you would repent of your sins

227
00:28:33,000 --> 00:28:39,000
and believe that Jesus is Lord, you will conquer. You will be a citizen of a new kingdom

228
00:28:39,000 --> 00:28:43,000
and a new throne. And this is the message of the gospel. It's so much bigger than where we go

229
00:28:43,000 --> 00:28:49,000
when we die. It transcends everything about us. We are now primarily citizens of a kingdom

230
00:28:49,000 --> 00:28:55,000
where Jesus is on the throne and death no longer has the last word. This is the message that we are

231
00:28:55,000 --> 00:29:01,000
called to proclaim. And if we get this message in our bones, we'll see it all over the place.

232
00:29:01,000 --> 00:29:08,000
We'll see invitations to partner with God, to share with others the good news of Jesus that transcends

233
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:15,000
not just where we go when we die, but everything about our lives. This is why we plan the series

234
00:29:15,000 --> 00:29:22,000
directly after Revelation. The message of the gospel is the declaration that Jesus is the one true Lord of the world.

235
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:28,000
He has won the decisive victory over evil and death itself. He has brought lasting peace and abundant life through his kingdom,

236
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:35,000
which is available to all. The dragon has been, do you remember, thrown down. The lamb is risen and reigning.

237
00:29:35,000 --> 00:29:41,000
All who repent of the dragon's false kingdoms and place their allegiance in Jesus as Lord are more than conquerors,

238
00:29:41,000 --> 00:29:47,000
and nothing can separate us from the love of the Father. The suffering of this present world is not worthy

239
00:29:47,000 --> 00:29:56,000
to be compared to the glory that will be revealed. And this is the message that we've been given to testify about.

240
00:29:56,000 --> 00:30:01,000
It isn't detached from our real lives. It isn't something that we just declare with our mouths,

241
00:30:01,000 --> 00:30:05,000
but we demonstrate with our lives in sacrificial love towards others.

242
00:30:05,000 --> 00:30:10,000
So we start by being moved with compassion, by clarifying the message.

243
00:30:10,000 --> 00:30:18,000
We understand that the gospel really is good news for the poor, the hurting, and the broken.

244
00:30:18,000 --> 00:30:23,000
And that fuels the way we go about evangelism.

245
00:30:23,000 --> 00:30:27,000
Colossians chapter 2 is where we're going to start to land the plane.

246
00:30:27,000 --> 00:30:33,000
Don't get too excited. I wish we were landing it too. It's fine. It's fine. Okay.

247
00:30:38,000 --> 00:30:44,000
I had a bookmark, but... All right. Colossians chapter 4 verse 2.

248
00:30:44,000 --> 00:30:48,000
Do we have it on the screen? Because I give you a different version than I have.

249
00:30:48,000 --> 00:30:56,000
Paul says, "Devote yourselves to prayer, being watchful and thankful, and pray for us too, that God may open a door for our message,

250
00:30:56,000 --> 00:31:06,000
so that we may proclaim the mystery of Christ, for which I am in chains. Pray that I may proclaim it clearly as I should.

251
00:31:06,000 --> 00:31:12,000
You, be wise in the way that you act towards outsiders, those who haven't tasted and seen the Lord is good.

252
00:31:12,000 --> 00:31:21,000
Make the most of every opportunity. Let your conversation be full of grace, seasoned with salt, so that you may know how to answer everyone."

253
00:31:21,000 --> 00:31:32,000
If we allow the Holy Spirit inside of us to develop true compassion for our neighbors, coworkers, and friends that haven't tasted and seen that the Lord is good.

254
00:31:32,000 --> 00:31:46,000
If we believe in our bones that the message of the kingdom of heaven is good news to the poor, the hurting, and the broken, then the way in which we go about sharing it is we prayerfully develop a right attitude.

255
00:31:46,000 --> 00:32:01,000
And we just creatively look for ways to share the good news to people we find ourselves in relationship with.

256
00:32:01,000 --> 00:32:06,000
I really just want to meditate on what it would look like for us to just do what Colossians says.

257
00:32:06,000 --> 00:32:18,000
To pray for people who are in our circles of influence, and to have compassion for them, and to demonstrate to them that you have compassion for them.

258
00:32:18,000 --> 00:32:27,000
Not fake compassion, not compassion that's motivated by some weird, like, spiritual quota that you're trying to hit, or some guilt that you're trying to avoid.

259
00:32:27,000 --> 00:32:32,000
Like, genuine compassion for people who have yet to taste and see the Lord is good.

260
00:32:32,000 --> 00:32:40,000
And then look for ways in which you can be a witness to that person, to the good news of the kingdom.

261
00:32:40,000 --> 00:32:48,000
And I think this is important, because I think it involves a lot more creativity than maybe it used to.

262
00:32:48,000 --> 00:32:55,000
I heard Tim Keller talk about this, and I'm just going to try and do my best to summarize what I heard him say,

263
00:32:55,000 --> 00:33:03,000
because he's Tim Keller and I'm not. Tim Keller was talking about the difference

264
00:33:03,000 --> 00:33:11,000
in how he did ministry and evangelize the people in the '80s in the rural Bible Belt

265
00:33:11,000 --> 00:33:16,000
versus when he moved to the heart of New York City in the 2000s.

266
00:33:16,000 --> 00:33:20,000
And he said that in the '80s, all he really had to do was connect the dots for people.

267
00:33:20,000 --> 00:33:24,000
So pretty much everybody in the Bible Belt in the '80s believed that there was a God,

268
00:33:24,000 --> 00:33:30,000
believed in some type of subjective morality and some type of afterlife.

269
00:33:30,000 --> 00:33:35,000
Even if they weren't familiar with the Bible, like they had enough of the concepts that he just had to step in and be like,

270
00:33:35,000 --> 00:33:38,000
"Oh, like this is where Jesus fits into these ideas that you already believe."

271
00:33:38,000 --> 00:33:44,000
And it was more like connecting the dots. It was easier in the '80s.

272
00:33:44,000 --> 00:33:51,000
And now he said most people, they've kind of lost a lot of those presuppositions.

273
00:33:51,000 --> 00:33:58,000
Whether we like it or not, this reality has spread, and an increasing number of people in our circles of influence,

274
00:33:58,000 --> 00:34:02,000
even here in Frederick County, I know we're still a little bit rural Bible Belt-ish,

275
00:34:02,000 --> 00:34:08,000
but it's still happening here that more and more people have less and less of those presuppositions.

276
00:34:08,000 --> 00:34:14,000
And here's where I want to be really, this is where I want to really put a flag in the ground.

277
00:34:14,000 --> 00:34:18,000
Our job is not to bemoan what used to be.

278
00:34:18,000 --> 00:34:25,000
Like, our job is not to try and fight a culture war to make the culture Christian again so that evangelism is easier.

279
00:34:25,000 --> 00:34:29,000
It's not our job. Our job is to have compassion for the people who are right in front of us.

280
00:34:29,000 --> 00:34:33,000
And the people who are right in front of us don't have the same presuppositions that they used to.

281
00:34:33,000 --> 00:34:37,000
And we could be upset about that. We could be frustrated about that. We could be discouraged about that.

282
00:34:37,000 --> 00:34:43,000
Or, with compassion, by the power of the Holy Spirit, we could think about how we could creatively demonstrate

283
00:34:43,000 --> 00:34:51,000
and declare the rule of King Jesus to them and invite them into a conversation about the one who is risen and reigning and on the throne.

284
00:34:51,000 --> 00:35:02,000
Because it's a lot easier to demonstrate and declare the kingdom of heaven in a culture that is not intertwined with evangelicalism.

285
00:35:02,000 --> 00:35:08,000
We can just demonstrate light is brighter when things are darker. Got it?

286
00:35:08,000 --> 00:35:17,000
So our job is not to bemoan what it used to be. It's to joyfully and creatively demonstrate and declare the rule of Jesus wherever we live, work, and play.

287
00:35:17,000 --> 00:35:32,000
Keller went on to explain that because people don't have these presuppositions, that what good evangelism looks like for them is instead of coming to a place where you connect all the dots and you say,

288
00:35:32,000 --> 00:35:41,000
"Do you want to receive Jesus as your Lord and Savior?" And they're like, "Yeah, great." That what evangelism looks like now more than ever is somebody has to say,

289
00:35:41,000 --> 00:35:51,000
"Oh, maybe not all Christians are judgmental hypocrites." Right? Like that's step one in the evangelism process is you become a person in their life who they say,

290
00:35:51,000 --> 00:36:01,000
"Oh, that person is a genuine Christian, and they're not judgmental or a hypocrite." Step one. He says step two might be something like,

291
00:36:01,000 --> 00:36:06,000
"They've built this relationship with you. They see you read your Bible. You talk to them a little bit, and they're like,

292
00:36:06,000 --> 00:36:14,000
"Oh, maybe the Bible isn't totally antiquated or irrelevant for my life." And that decision leads them to interact with the teachings of Jesus,

293
00:36:14,000 --> 00:36:19,000
which might cause them to be spiritually curious, which gets the snowball rolling down the hill,

294
00:36:19,000 --> 00:36:24,000
which might lead them to begin to pray on their own, see God eventually through the work of the Holy Spirit,

295
00:36:24,000 --> 00:36:30,000
get to that place where they repent and believe the good news of Jesus. But evangelism looks

296
00:36:30,000 --> 00:36:37,000
different today than it did when most of us were given tools of how to do it. And what I want to give us

297
00:36:37,000 --> 00:36:42,000
freedom to do is just, by the power of the Holy Spirit, think creatively and gracefully about how we can

298
00:36:42,000 --> 00:36:46,000
demonstrate and declare the rule and reign of Jesus to people all around us who don't have the

299
00:36:46,000 --> 00:36:52,000
presuppositions that they used to have. If evangelism used to be about connecting the dots, I think now

300
00:36:52,000 --> 00:36:58,000
it's much more about opening doors and inviting people to come in and see the room.

301
00:36:59,000 --> 00:37:05,000
It's hard work. It involves creativity. But if we've developed a heart of compassion for people who don't know Jesus,

302
00:37:05,000 --> 00:37:11,000
and if we truly understand how good the message of Jesus is, we will joyfully look for opportunities

303
00:37:11,000 --> 00:37:16,000
to open the doors of the kingdom for others to step in and see God's goodness.

304
00:37:16,000 --> 00:37:20,000
So let's kind of land the plane, get some application in.

305
00:37:20,000 --> 00:37:28,000
We've got to understand the culture, beliefs, and story of the people in our lives. This is the first work of

306
00:37:28,000 --> 00:37:34,000
every good missionary. Most good missionaries you talk to say that they're not even looking to have a spiritual

307
00:37:34,000 --> 00:37:39,000
conversation with somebody until like three to five years into living there, because they're transporting

308
00:37:39,000 --> 00:37:43,000
themselves into a totally different culture that they do not understand, and to a people that do not

309
00:37:43,000 --> 00:37:48,000
trust them. And so the beautiful thing about where we're at, where we live, work, and play, is you know

310
00:37:48,000 --> 00:37:53,000
the culture, you know the people in front of you, but you've got to understand their story. You've got to

311
00:37:53,000 --> 00:37:57,000
listen to them. Do not come into a spiritual conversation with somebody assuming you know anything

312
00:37:57,000 --> 00:38:03,000
about somebody. Ask them. Ask them questions. We don't have to awkwardly read off a script. We can

313
00:38:03,000 --> 00:38:07,000
start with intentionally loving people God has placed in our lives with no strings attached. You

314
00:38:07,000 --> 00:38:13,000
can love somebody and never get to have a spiritual conversation with them. We're sowing seeds of the

315
00:38:13,000 --> 00:38:20,000
gospel in their lives. Do we trust the God who rose from the dead, that he is sovereign over the

316
00:38:20,000 --> 00:38:26,000
souls of individuals, or do we really think it's our job to make it happen? We become a trustworthy

317
00:38:26,000 --> 00:38:31,000
person who begins to witness to the good news through our words and our actions. We don't

318
00:38:31,000 --> 00:38:35,000
put pressure on ourselves to close the deal. We trust that God is at work, and we position ourselves

319
00:38:35,000 --> 00:38:41,000
to partner with his Holy Spirit. The tension that I forgot to mention between demonstrate

320
00:38:41,000 --> 00:38:49,000
and declare, there is a tension there. The Bible does tell us to declare the gospel, and

321
00:38:49,000 --> 00:38:55,000
there's a type of evangelism where people just want to do the demonstrating. They just want to be loving,

322
00:38:55,000 --> 00:38:59,000
compassionate people towards people who don't yet know Jesus, and they never want to tell them that,

323
00:38:59,000 --> 00:39:05,000
like, Jesus said, "I am the way, the truth, and the life," because that sounds hurtful. And there's a

324
00:39:05,000 --> 00:39:11,000
different kind of people that just want to declare and never want to demonstrate. Also bad. We're

325
00:39:11,000 --> 00:39:17,000
trying to thread the needle here. Sound good? All right. So my main point this morning is, followers

326
00:39:17,000 --> 00:39:24,000
of Jesus, we get to joyfully and creatively witness to the kingdom of God when we develop compassion for

327
00:39:24,000 --> 00:39:30,000
others and are convinced that the gospel is actually good news. The solution to bad evangelism isn't no

328
00:39:30,000 --> 00:39:36,000
evangelism. It starts with our motivation, is developed by our understanding of the message, and will

329
00:39:36,000 --> 00:39:40,000
ultimately impact how we go about doing it. So if you want to get really practical, if you want to do

330
00:39:40,000 --> 00:39:46,000
something, if you want homework, I know you guys want homework, school's coming back into session. I'd say

331
00:39:46,000 --> 00:39:52,000
this, write down the names of three to five people in your life who don't follow Jesus. Just begin to

332
00:39:52,000 --> 00:39:57,920
to notice things about them. What do you already know about their lives? And what have you assumed

333
00:39:57,920 --> 00:40:04,320
about their lives? Ask them questions about their beliefs, about their presuppositions, and ask to

334
00:40:04,320 --> 00:40:09,520
listen, not to respond. So often when we do get the courage to have these conversations, we feel like

335
00:40:09,520 --> 00:40:16,160
it's our job in the first moment to tell them why their presuppositions are wrong. Listen, compassion,

336
00:40:16,160 --> 00:40:23,360
be moved towards them, pray for them, and ultimately entrust the outcome to God.

